      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct answer.\n26-30: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Dr Ruth Nair: Our caption-lag proposal needs one clean manipulation. We must hold wording constant, select meaningful outcomes and prevent practice order from biasing the comparison.\nMina Duarte: The first design changed both timing and wording accuracy, so any effect could not be attributed to caption lag alone.\nDr Ruth Nair: The study asks how caption timing affects learning behaviour, not whether captions are generally helpful or harmful.\nMina Duarte: Note quality is scored by two readers who do not know which timing condition produced each page.\nJacob Pell: The two blind note scorers resolve disagreements through the rubric and record their initial ratings before discussion.\nJacob Pell: Unfamiliar topics reduce the advantage held by students who already know the content.\nMina Duarte: Both recordings use the same speaker, visual style and approximate information density, but cover different unfamiliar subjects.\nDr Ruth Nair: Analysis will consider whether delayed captions change which ideas are written down, not merely the total number of notes.\nDr Ruth Nair: We will rotate recording order so the second condition does not always benefit from practice.\nJacob Pell: A transcript corrected after the session would not recreate the real-time experience, so all outcomes are captured immediately.\nDr Ruth Nair: The interview asks about strategies, such as switching attention between slides and captions, without implying that one strategy is correct.\nMina Duarte: Could we add eye tracking? On reflection, calibration could consume too much of each session in a project this small.\nMina Duarte: Alongside the comprehension test, we will score the quality of participants’ notes using a blind rubric.\nDr Ruth Nair: The scoring rubric rewards accurate ideas and useful organisation rather than the sheer number of words written.\nMina Duarte: The team will report missing data caused by equipment failure separately from blank responses supplied by participants.\nJacob Pell: I will log pauses or restarts separately, since replaying material would change the one-pass comparison we intend to study.\nJacob Pell: Concentration ratings are useful background data, but they cannot be the only outcome because they are subjective.\nMina Duarte: Students with hearing needs are not excluded, but the team will record preferred access arrangements and avoid treating them as a single group.\nJacob Pell: I will synchronise caption and audio tracks using a visible marker before exporting the files for the experiment.\nDr Ruth Nair: Participants see a neutral screen during the final questionnaire, preventing one caption condition from remaining visible as they answer.\nDr Ruth Nair: Recruit first-year students so experience with recorded university lectures is reasonably similar.\nJacob Pell: Mina will prepare the delayed files, I will randomise the sequence, and Dr Nair will check that the comparison changes only timing.\nDr Ruth Nair: Mina checks each delayed file from beginning to end because a correct opening does not guarantee stable timing later.\nMina Duarte: The consent process explains that reading speed is not being diagnosed and individual scores will not be returned as an ability test.\nMina Duarte: The delayed condition will trail the speech by three seconds; five seconds felt obviously disruptive in the pilot.\nDr Ruth Nair: One recording concerns an unfamiliar geological process and the other a historical research method, reducing direct overlap in background knowledge.\nMina Duarte: Dr Nair rejects a live internet stream, which could introduce buffering delays unrelated to the planned caption condition.\nJacob Pell: Closed-back headphones will reduce variation from corridor noise.\nMina Duarte: Caption wording will be identical in both conditions; only the time at which each caption appears will change.\nJacob Pell: The testing room uses identical screens, and automatic caption controls are disabled so participants cannot alter presentation settings.\nDr Ruth Nair: Participants will take notes on paper, avoiding differences in laptop notification settings.\nJacob Pell: The three-second lag was chosen because pilot students noticed disruption without abandoning the caption stream entirely.\nJacob Pell: Participants keep the same paper size and pen colour so page images do not reveal the condition to scorers.\nDr Ruth Nair: A brief practice clip teaches the note-taking procedure but contains no material from either scored lecture.\nJacob Pell: A second pilot checks whether the paper note sheet offers enough space without encouraging unusually detailed transcription.\nMina Duarte: The final interview will last five minutes and ask when participants noticed the delay.\nDr Ruth Nair: Comprehension questions target ideas stated in speech rather than incidental details visible on a slide.\nMina Duarte: Students answer comprehension items before the interview to prevent later discussion from changing their recorded understanding.\nJacob Pell: The caption study now isolates timing, combines comprehension with blinded note scoring and documents how participants adapt their attention.",
