      "title": "Listening — Part 4: Seagrass carbon burial",
      "instructions": "31-36: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n37-40: Choose the correct answer.",
      "transcript": "Professor Nia Mercer: Today I will use a coastal-restoration case study from our methods course. Its meadow records and carbon figures belong to the exercise; focus on how the accounting works.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: In the first sample, the leaf canopy slows water near the seabed, allowing material to settle.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Carbon capture, burial and long-term storage describe related but distinct stages, and using the words interchangeably creates misleading estimates.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Protection claims should distinguish avoided loss from new burial, because both matter but answer different management questions.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Meadows trap fine particles carried from elsewhere as well as material produced inside the habitat.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Leaves reduce current speed, allowing particles to settle, while below-ground structures make the surface less easily disturbed.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Burial does not guarantee permanence because oxygen exposure, erosion or disturbance can return stored carbon to active cycles.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Bulk density measurements convert concentration into stock per area; using concentration alone can misrepresent loosely packed layers.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Long-term monitoring reveals whether early gains persist after storms, plant turnover and changes in surrounding land use.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: In this dataset, most long-term carbon lies in sediment, not visible leaves.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: The next section separates sources: some buried carbon comes from the meadow, while some arrives from rivers, nearby habitats or offshore water.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Fine sediment can contain more organic material than coarse sand, so changing grain size across sites may influence concentration estimates.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: The survey team records meadow boundaries because expanding the sampled area would raise estimated stock without changing the study meadow.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Public summaries should avoid converting uncertain stocks directly into guaranteed climate benefits without showing the assumptions used.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: In our course meadow, roots and rhizomes form a network that helps stabilise the seabed.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: A large existing stock does not reveal how quickly it formed or how much would remain if the meadow were damaged.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Below ground, roots and rhizomes contribute material directly while binding deposits against ordinary current movement.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: For the methods workshop, compare two independent dating estimates and flag any disagreement rather than accepting one isolated date.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Root, rhizome and leaf tissues differ in chemistry, so their contributions to sediment carbon cannot be treated as identical.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Even careful carbon estimates sit alongside other restoration outcomes, including habitat, water quality and shoreline stability.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: We now examine sampling. A sediment core provides a vertical sequence for analysis by depth.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Cores provide depth profiles, but compression, mixing by animals and dating uncertainty complicate interpretation.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Imported material may already be old, so not all buried carbon counts as newly captured.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Boat anchoring and dredging disturb sediment directly, whereas nutrient stress may weaken vegetation before erosion becomes visible.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: The team uses coded chemical markers to estimate when the sampled layers accumulated.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Storms can remove vegetation and expose sediment, potentially remobilising material that had been buried.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: A core is divided into depth intervals so concentration, particle size and potential age markers can be compared vertically.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: A reference meadow helps reveal natural year-to-year variation that could otherwise be mistaken for a restoration effect.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Restoration plots can fail unevenly, so averaging planted and bare patches requires a transparent area-weighting method.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Carbon produced by the seagrass itself is classified as local plant material.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: For evaluation, the study compares pre-intervention measurements with reference sites.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Restored plants may stabilise sediment before measurable carbon accumulation becomes large, so ecological recovery and storage rates need separate reporting.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: A nearby unvegetated site is informative but may differ in current exposure or sediment source from the restored meadow.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Particles washed from the catchment belong to the imported terrestrial category.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: The report includes uncertainty ranges because its short monitoring period cannot demonstrate permanent storage.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Damage can expose deep layers rapidly, demonstrating that stock protection may matter as much as annual sequestration.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: In the field plan, cores follow a pre-set sampling grid and any disturbance caused by sampling is recorded.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: In the storm record, carbon lost after a severe event is recorded as remobilised material because its burial has been reversed.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Seagrass photosynthesis fixes carbon into plant tissue, but much of that visible tissue is short-lived and may be consumed or exported.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Management estimates distinguish gross accumulation from additional storage attributable to the intervention.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: For the final reporting stage, the account specifies depth because a shallow survey cannot be compared directly with a much deeper historical core.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Additional storage after restoration is the restoration gain, calculated against a credible baseline rather than the total stock.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: The dense canopy reduces turbulence near the bed, creating conditions in which suspended mineral and organic particles can settle.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: The report states its measurement boundaries, origin assumptions and the period over which continued burial is observed.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: Laboratory analysis can identify broad material sources, although mixed origins rarely separate into perfectly distinct categories.\nProfessor Nia Mercer: For the restoration scenario, report source, depth, baseline and permanence instead of equating existing sediment stock with a guaranteed new benefit.",
